<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Orange Revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2004">2004</a>–<a href="/years/2005">2005</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a> · Also known as Pomarancheva revoliutsiia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Orange Revolution was a series of protests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> from late November 2004 to January 2005, set off by the presidential run-off of 21 November 2004 between Viktor Yushchenko and Viktor Yanukovych, which election monitors and much of the public held to have been rigged in Yanukovych's favour. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> was the centre of a campaign of civil resistance, with daily demonstrations, sit-ins and general strikes across the country. Ukraine's Supreme Court annulled the result and ordered a new vote for 26 December 2004, which observers judged free and fair; Yushchenko won about 52% to Yanukovych's 44% and was inaugurated on 23 January 2005, ending the revolution.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/orange-revolution.jpg" alt="Orange Revolution" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Serhiy · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morning_first_day_of_Orange_Revolution.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Ukrainian band GreenJolly reworked it as &quot;Razom nas bahato&quot; (Together We Are Many) in the first days of the <b>Orange Revolution</b> of 2004, and that song was Ukraine's entry at the Eurovision Song Contest of 2005.</blockquote><a href="/songs/el-pueblo-unido-jamas-sera-vencido">El pueblo unido jamás será vencido</a></li><li><blockquote>The anthem became especially popular after the <b>Orange Revolution</b> of 2004 and the Euromaidan protests of 2013, when protesters, led by the singer Ruslana, sang it once an hour in the early weeks; Nadia Diuk called it the clarion call of that revolution.</blockquote><a href="/songs/national-anthem-of-ukraine">State Anthem of Ukraine</a></li></ul></section>
